---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { LAYERS, READING_ORDER, DOC_COUNT } from '../data/nav';
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION } from '../consts';
import pathwayData from '../data/pathways.json';

const first = READING_ORDER[0];

/*
  Entry points, from src/data/pathways.json. Four of them are doors onto the
  four routes that already exist inside getting_started.md — the routes name
  SECTIONS rather than documents, and they stay in the document because that is
  where their caveats live ("each route is in priority order", "the one-line
  descriptions are signposts, not summaries", "no route replaces asking your
  club"). Duplicating ~90 deep anchors into site data would copy the list and
  leave the caveats behind.

  ⚠️ status 'draft' MUST NOT RENDER. TWO pathways are draft today, and an earlier
  version of this very comment said "one" — which was false, eleven lines above a
  comment that exists solely to record that an earlier comment in this file was
  false. A commit-gate caught it.

    · parent-of-a-new-player — the U10/parent entry point the plan requires. The
      corpus has the material and no route written for that reader, and a label
      describing a U10 reader holds only after a named safety reviewer has verified
      the jurisdiction, contact rules, equipment assumptions and supervision context.
    · core-principles — the document this release created. It is draft until fresh
      reviewers have read the document itself, not the pathway.

  Filtering here rather than omitting the rows keeps both gaps visible in the data.
  ⚠️ Count the drafts in pathways.json before editing this comment; do not trust it.
*/
const pathways = pathwayData.pathways.filter((p) => p.status === 'reviewed');

/*
  ⚠️ The Getting Started layer is deliberately NOT rendered in the catalogue below.
  It is a one-document section, and every one of the entry points above lands inside
  that very document — four of the five link to its own route anchors — while the hero
  button reads "Start with Getting Started". Rendering it again as a lone catalogue card
  was the same door a third time. Owner's instruction, 14 September 2026.

  ⚠️ IT IS REMOVED FROM THIS PAGE'S CATALOGUE ONLY. The document is untouched, keeps its
  route, and is still in structure.json, nav.ts, the sidebar on every page, PrevNext,
  search, the sitemap and the RSS and podcast feeds. DOC_COUNT is computed from nav.ts,
  so "Search all N documents" is unaffected and still counts it. Do not "fix" the
  apparent mismatch between the hero's count and the number of cards rendered here.
*/
const catalogue = LAYERS.filter((layer) => layer.id !== 'getting-started');
const hrefFor = (p: (typeof pathwayData.pathways)[number]) =>
  'doc' in p.entry ? `/${p.entry.doc}/#${p.entry.anchor}` : `/${p.entry.layer}/`;
---

<Base description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} bare={true}>
  <main class="main" id="main">
    <section class="home-hero">
      <h1>Ice hockey — learning to play the game</h1>
      {/*
        The visible lede is deliberately NOT {SITE_DESCRIPTION} any more. That const is
        still this page's meta/OG description via the Base prop above, where a search
        result needs one tight sentence; this is the on-page pitch, which needs room.
        Keep them separate — the podcast feed's description was split off from
        SITE_DESCRIPTION for the same reason, and see the note in consts.ts.
      */}
      <p>
        Learn Ice Hockey is a complete learning system for understanding the game at a
        deeper level — not just what to do, but why it works. It’s a structured knowledge
        base that teaches players how to move, how to execute, how to read developing
        plays, and how five skaters operate as a single, connected unit.
      </p>
      <p>
        The entire site is built on five distinct on-ice layers, and on showing how the
        layers fit together:
      </p>
      <ul class="home-hero__layers">
        <li>Position without systems leads to following rules you don’t truly understand.</li>
        <li>Systems without technique means knowing where to be but not being able to get there.</li>
        <li>
          Technique and systems without hockey IQ means executing the wrong thing at the
          right moment.
        </li>
      </ul>
      {/*
        These five are the site's own ON-ICE layers, in structure.json's order. ⚠️ THEY ARE
        NOT ALL THE SECTIONS RENDERED BELOW, and an earlier version of this comment said
        they were — which was false and would have told the next editor the page was
        consistent when it was not. ⚠️ UPDATED 14 September 2026: SIX sections render here,
        not seven. Getting Started was dropped from the catalogue on the owner's
        instruction because the entry points above lead into it three times over, so
        Off the Ice is now the ONLY rendered section that is not an on-ice layer, and the
        site says so in its own tagline: "Underneath all five layers". Getting Started's
        tagline, "Before everything else", is still live everywhere the layer still
        appears — the sidebar, search, the sitemap, the feeds. The word
        "on-ice" in the paragraph above is what makes the count true, and the previous lede
        carried it. A draft that dropped it left the page claiming five while rendering
        seven. Do not remove it.

        The other way to get this wrong, already made once: that draft listed "team
        cohesion" as a layer, which the site does not have, and dropped foundation, which
        is the section the page itself labels "Read these first".
      */}
      <p>
        By breaking the sport into its true components — foundation, positioning, systems,
        technique, and hockey IQ — we try to give players a complete mental model of the
        game.
      </p>
      <div class="home-cta">
        <a class="button" href={first.href}>Start with {first.title}</a>
        <a class="button button--ghost" href="/search/">Search all {DOC_COUNT} documents</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="layer-section" id="start-here">
      <div class="layer-section__head">
        <h2>Where do you want to start?</h2>
        {/* Counted from what actually renders, not written as a literal — one pathway is
            draft and filtered out above, and a hard-coded number would have been wrong. */}
        <span class="layer-section__tagline">{pathways.length} ways in</span>
      </div>
      <p class="layer-section__blurb">
        Each of these lands you in the middle of a document rather than at the top of one,
        because what you need before Saturday is a handful of sections, not a reading list.
        Everything else is below, and the full contents stay one click away from every page.
      </p>
      <div class="card-grid">
        {
          pathways.map((p) => (
            <a class="card" href={hrefFor(p)}>
              <span class="card__title">{p.title}</span>
              <span class="card__desc">{p.blurb}</span>
              <span class="card__meta">{p.effort}</span>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    {
      catalogue.map((layer) => (
        <section class="layer-section" id={layer.id}>
          <div class="layer-section__head">
            <h2>{layer.title}</h2>
            <span class="layer-section__tagline">{layer.tagline}</span>
          </div>
          <p class="layer-section__blurb">{layer.blurb}</p>
          <div class="card-grid">
            {layer.docs.map((doc) => (
              <a class="card" href={doc.href}>
                <span class="card__title">{doc.title}</span>
                <span class="card__desc">{doc.description}</span>
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>
      ))
    }
  </main>
</Base>
